{"id":5923,"date":"2022-03-02T14:05:03","date_gmt":"2022-03-02T22:05:03","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=5923"},"modified":"2025-10-23T13:42:15","modified_gmt":"2025-10-23T20:42:15","slug":"3-7-color-sensor-cde-blockly","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/3-7-color-sensor-cde-blockly\/","title":{"rendered":"3.7 &#8211; Color Sensor"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22138300beb\" name=\"6a22138300beb\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>We've Moved!<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text css=&#8221;.vc_custom_1761252133432{padding-top: 20px !important;padding-right: 20px !important;padding-bottom: 20px !important;padding-left: 20px !important;background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}&#8221;]<\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"alignleft wp-image-11766\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/06\/icon_warning-150x150-2-84x85.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"81\" height=\"82\" \/><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\"><strong>We\u2019ve moved to our new lesson portal: <a href=\"http:\/\/learn.robolink.com\">Robolink Learn<\/a>! Lessons here will remain available until the end of June 2026. This version of Basecamp will no longer be updated and uses outdated references to Blockly for Robolink. You can access the previous version of <a href=\"https:\/\/codrone.robolink.com\/edu\/blockly-archive\/\">Blockly here<\/a>.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22138300d47\" name=\"6a22138300d47\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Goals and Steps to Success<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Goals:<\/p>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, I will be able to:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Know the difference between HSV and RGB<\/li>\n<li>Describe how the CoDrone EDU classifies colors<\/li>\n<li>Calibrate my color sensor<\/li>\n<li>Predict colors with the CoDrone EDU<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Steps to Success:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Color Sensor Calibration<\/li>\n<li>Color Sensor Prediction<\/li>\n<li>Color Piano<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 1: Multiple Samples<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 2: Color Piano &#8211; Extended<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<a id=\"6a22138300dca\" name=\"6a22138300dca\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>CoDrone EDU Color Sensors<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div>\n<div>You can see colors and differentiate between them, but did you know robots can do this too? In this lesson, you will learn how to use the CoDrone EDU\u2019s color sensor to distinguish between different colors. The CoDrone EDU is equipped with two color sensors on the bottom of the drone. When your CoDrone EDU is powered on, you may have noticed the two bright lights projected underneath the drone. Those lights are to help your color sensors detect how much red, green, and blue light are being reflected.<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;10626&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<strong>Important:<\/strong> The color sensors will only work if the drone has landed on a surface. The color sensors <em>cannot<\/em> detect the surface underneath during flight.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;10627&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22138300e4e\" name=\"6a22138300e4e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Using HSV instead of RGB<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<p class=\"\" data-source-line=\"25\">Your drone will return color data in the <strong>HSV<\/strong> color space. HSV stands for hue, saturation, and value.<\/p>\n<ul data-source-line=\"26\">\n<li class=\"\" data-source-line=\"26\"><strong>Hue<\/strong>\u00a0represents the color as a value from 0-360<\/li>\n<li class=\"\" data-source-line=\"27\"><strong>Saturation<\/strong>\u00a0is the color\u2019s intensity from 0-100<\/li>\n<li class=\"\" data-source-line=\"28\"><strong>Value<\/strong>\u00a0is the lightness or darkness of the color from 0-100<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"highlight-line\" data-source-line=\"30\">For computer recognition and other robotics applications, it is better to use the HSV color space, since it separates values for colors and intensity. This is important because shadows, reflections, and other factors may cause certain colors to look very different. The HSV color space takes this into account for more accurate results.<\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2102&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22138300eb5\" name=\"6a22138300eb5\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Reading Color Data from the Controller<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Let\u2019s start by finding how to access color data from the remote controller. <strong>First, make sure that you are not in the \u201cLINK\u201d state.<\/strong> To access color data from the controller, we need to be in remote control flight mode. Once in the remote control state, press either the \u201cP\u201d or \u201cS\u201d button until you see the screen in the image below. From this screen you can see HSV values (Hue, Saturation, Value) as well as the current color seen by the drone![\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;6916&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Place the drone on top of each of the 8 color cards that you received in the box.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Note:<\/strong> If the color is predicted incorrectly or return an unknown color, you can calibrate the sensor by following the instructions in <a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/0-6-calibrating-the-color-sensor-jrotc\/\">this lesson<\/a>. Although this lesson is for the JROTC edition of the drone, it applies to CoDrone EDU.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22138300f7a\" name=\"6a22138300f7a\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Training Color Data<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Click on the circular icon that is on the right side of Blockly to open the color training tool. Next, click on the <strong>New color data<\/strong> button to start color training process.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13506&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]The color training tool will prompt you to enter a name for your color data set.\u00a0 Name your color set <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">codrone_colors<\/code>, or any other name that you will remember.<\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13507&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]To train your color data, you need to collect color samples for each color that you have in mind (indigo, orange, beige, red, black, etc.). To simplify what colors we are going to be collecting data from, we will use the color cards provided by the CoDrone EDU kit.<\/p>\n<p>The color training tool will prompt you to add a color to your data set by clicking the &#8216;+&#8217; button.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13508&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Now, you can add a name for the color that you just added! Let&#8217;s start with &#8216;red&#8217; first.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13509&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]After entering a name for your color, you can now click on the color picker icon right above the name to open a pop-up window to start collecting samples.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13510&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]A pop-up window will appear that will let you collect samples of the desired color. The window provides the name of your color data set, the name of the color that you will be collecting, instructions on how to properly collect samples, and a button to collect samples.<\/p>\n<p>Once your drone is flat on the surface and its color sensors start illuminating, you can click on &#8216;Collect samples&#8217;.<\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13511&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<p class=\"\" data-source-line=\"61\">Now, you can repeat the same process for the other color cards!<\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13512&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]The color training tool tab should look like this once you finish collecting samples for each color![\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13513&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221;][vc_column_text]If you&#8217;re unhappy with how a color turned out, you can click on the &#8216;X&#8217; button that&#8217;s next to one of the colored circles to remove it from your color data set and simply add a new color with the same name. For example, the &#8216;red&#8217; color data that we collected didn&#8217;t look red, so the &#8216;red&#8217; color was removed and was redone. Remember, if all of your colors aren&#8217;t coming out the way that they&#8217;re supposed to, head to <a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/0-6-calibrating-the-color-sensor-jrotc\/\">this lesson<\/a> to re-calibrate your drone&#8217;s color sensors![\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13514&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221;][vc_column_text]Now, let&#8217;s start testing our color data set! Click on the red &#8216;Test color data&#8221; to test your color data set. This will open a pop-up window.<\/p>\n<p>In this pop-up window, the window will show you the actual color that your color sensors are detecting, the HSV value of the detected color, and a prediction of the name of the detected color. This prediction (or guess) is based off the color data set that you provided to Blockly.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13516&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221;][vc_column_text]The process that we went through previously of collecting samples of color data and giving it a name (labeling) is called &#8220;training&#8221;. You&#8217;re essentially telling the program (Blockly, in this case) that &#8220;these samples of color data that I collected should be considered as the color red&#8221; (or whatever color)! During the training phase, you can name the color any way you want, mahogany, orange, pink, etc. It all depends on the context, like the color of the surface you&#8217;re using to collect data from.<\/p>\n<p>Additionally, if you were to place the drone on a surface that wasn&#8217;t used in the training phase, during the testing phase, Blockly will give you its closest guess on what the name of the detected color is.[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Make sure you&#8217;ve tested your color data set to make sure it is providing the correct guesses by placing your drone on each color card.<\/p>\n<p>If it isn&#8217;t giving correct guesses, click on the &#8216;X&#8217; button on the top-right to modify your color data set. Otherwise, it is highly recommended to download your color data set with the gray button before moving on.<\/p>\n<p>Let&#8217;s click <strong>Finish and create blocks<\/strong>! This will populate blocks in the &#8220;Colors&#8221; category located in the &#8220;Senior&#8221; tab.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13515&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221;][vc_column_text]If you forget to download your color data set, don&#8217;t worry! There&#8217;s a download button that will appear on the top-right side of the color training tool. Right next to the download button, there&#8217;s a trash can icon to remove the existing color data set and depopulate the &#8220;Colors&#8221; category.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13517&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221;][vc_column_text css=&#8221;.vc_custom_1743727046704{padding-top: 20px !important;padding-right: 20px !important;padding-bottom: 20px !important;padding-left: 20px !important;background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}&#8221;]<\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-11766 alignleft\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/06\/icon_warning-150x150-2-84x85.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"106\" height=\"107\" \/><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\"><strong>Save your data!<\/strong><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\">Although Blockly will save your blocks in the workspace whenever possible, color data files cannot currently be saved in the browser. Download your color data if you want to reuse it in a later program.<\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Blockly will add new blocks for you in the &#8220;Colors&#8221; menu. There will be a new block available for every color that you trained. Open the menu now to see these blocks. Your color training is complete and ready to use![\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13518&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22138301008\" name=\"6a22138301008\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Predicting Colors from a Data Set<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text css=&#8221;.vc_custom_1747695933681{padding-top: 20px !important;padding-right: 20px !important;padding-bottom: 20px !important;padding-left: 20px !important;background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}&#8221;]<\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-11766 alignleft\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/06\/icon_warning-150x150-2-84x85.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"106\" height=\"107\" \/><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\"><strong>Refresh warning!<\/strong><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\">Although Blockly will save your blocks in the workspace whenever possible, color data files cannot currently be saved in the browser. <strong>If you refresh the page, you will need to create or upload the color data.<\/strong> To learn how to upload color data, read step 10.<\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]In this example, you will learn how to use the\u00a0<strong><code>predict_color()<\/code><\/strong> with your dataset. From the &#8220;Colors&#8221; menu, drag the\u00a0<strong><code>load_color_data()<\/code><\/strong>\u00a0block into your workspace with the dataset you just created above.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13200&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]In a loop that repeats forever (while code is running), create a variable that will store the predicted color returned from\u00a0<strong><code>predict_color()<\/code><\/strong>. You can only use the green\u00a0<strong><code>predict_color()<\/code><\/strong>\u00a0block together with\u00a0<strong><code>load_color_data()<\/code><\/strong>. Finally, print the color to the console and wait 0.5 seconds. To test, run the code while placing the drone on each color card.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13201&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22138301075\" name=\"6a22138301075\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Color Piano<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In this activity, you will modify your code to make your drone into an instrument! Every color will make a unique buzzer noise when it is detected. To do this, add a conditional statement that compares the\u00a0<strong><code>predicted_color<\/code><\/strong> with a color label. You can find the color labels in the &#8220;Colors&#8221; menu. If the predicted color matches a designated color (like red), play a sound for a half a second![\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13202&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<p class=\"highlight-line\" data-source-line=\"91\">To add more colors, click the blue gear icon and add more\u00a0<strong><code>else if<\/code><\/strong> blocks. <em>How many colors can you add?<\/em><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13203&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]As you add more conditional statements, remember that you can only use the labels that are in your dataset.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13519&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22138301122\" name=\"6a22138301122\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Saving your code and data<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<strong>Saving code and saving data are separate!<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>If you use the &#8220;Save&#8221; option from the menu, it will only save the blocks that are in your workspace. In this case, it will only save the &#8220;Color Piano&#8221; program to your computer as an .xml file that you can load later.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13520&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]\u26a0\ufe0f <strong>This option will not save your color data set. You must use the download option when you create the data set or after creating the data set in the color training tool.<\/strong>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]If you forgot to download your color data, you can click the download button on the top-right side of the color training tool (a color data set must already exist in Blockly for this option to show).[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13517&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221383011b6\" name=\"6a221383011b6\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 10<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Uploading and loading previous data<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]If you have previously saved some code with custom color data, you will need to follow the following steps.<\/p>\n<p><strong>1. Upload the code\u00a0<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Blockly code is saved as an .XML file. If you try to open it with a text editor on your computer, it will not look like familiar Block code. To see any code you have saved, you have to open it in the Blockly workspace.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13522&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<strong>2. Upload the data folder<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>When Blockly reads the &#8220;load color data&#8221; block, it is expecting to reference the color data that you collected. If your &#8220;Colors&#8221; menu is empty, then there is no data for your code to use! Click on &#8220;Load color data&#8221;.<\/p>\n<p><strong>NOTE:<\/strong> Before clicking on &#8220;Upload color data&#8221; to upload your color data set from your computer, make sure that the color data set is not a ZIP file. The ZIP file must be extracted, so your color data set is in the form of a folder.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13521&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]\u26a0\ufe0f Make sure the name of the folder that you upload <strong>matches the name of the data set<\/strong> that your code is trying to load. You can find this name in the &#8220;load color data&#8221; block.<\/p>\n<p><strong> If you upload the incorrect folder, your code will not be able to find the data.<\/strong>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13468&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22138301221\" name=\"6a22138301221\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 11<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenges<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<p id=\"light-up-piano\" data-source-line=\"101\"><strong>Light-up Piano<\/strong><\/p>\n<p class=\"\" data-source-line=\"102\">Add to your program and make the LEDs change color to match the color being detected.<\/p>\n<p id=\"custom-landing-pads\" data-source-line=\"104\"><strong>Custom Landing Pads<\/strong><\/p>\n<p class=\"highlight-line\" data-source-line=\"105\">Grab some markers and art supplies to design your own landing pads! Pick some new colors and use the color sensor calibration program to detect new colors.<\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213830127d\" name=\"6a2213830127d\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 12<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Lesson Complete<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div>\n<div>In this lesson, you learned how to use your CoDrone EDU\u2019s color sensor to detect different colors. Using this data, you learned how to calibrate your drone to predict what color is being detected.<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;7547&#8243; img_size=&#8221;750&#215;670&#8243; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 13 We've Moved! [vc_column_text css=\".vc_custom_1761252133432{padding-top: 20px !important;padding-right: 20px !important;padding-bottom: 20px !important;padding-left: 20px !important;background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}\"] We\u2019ve moved to our new lesson portal: Robolink Learn! Lessons here&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/3-7-color-sensor-cde-blockly\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4742,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"easy","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3877,"_lesson_preview":""},"lesson-category":[],"lesson-tag":[2108,4328,14401,2189],"class_list":["post-5923","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-blockly","lesson-tag-cde","lesson-tag-cdej","lesson-tag-sensors","module-advanced","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/5923","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history"